#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""memory-sync — keep doctrine-grade memories identical across Jeff's Claude profiles.

Four profiles keep independent memory stores that drift:

    ~/.claude/projects/-Users-jeffpinto/memory/            (main)
    ~/.claude-claudine/projects/-Users-jeffpinto/memory/   (claudine)
    ~/.claude-claudette/projects/-Users-jeffpinto/memory/  (claudette)
    ~/.claude-claudeux/projects/-Users-jeffpinto/memory/   (claudeux, registered 2026-09-07)

Each store is one-fact-per-file markdown with YAML-ish frontmatter, plus a
MEMORY.md index that is loaded into every session of that profile. A doctrine
rule learned in one profile is invisible to the others until someone copies
it by hand (2026-08-26: persona-check-is-rule-zero, hand-copied three ways,
and the copies were not even byte-identical. 2026-09-06 MEM-2: claudeux was
missing from PROFILES entirely, so three feedback memories the binding
global CLAUDE.md cites by identifier -- status-updates-exec-summary-20min,
needs-you-is-standalone, repeat-shortfall-means-automate -- existed only in
claudeux and were invisible to every other profile's sync).

Registered profiles are asserted complete by `--check`: any ~/.claude* store
directory (main included) that carries a memory index but isn't in PROFILES
is a registry gap, not silent -- see `unregistered_profile_dirs()`.

WHAT SYNCS ("doctrine-grade") — derived by reading all 134 memories, not assumed:

  type: feedback  -> rules about HOW to work with Jeff. Profile-agnostic by
                     construction; a missing one makes a profile repeat a
                     correction Jeff already paid for once.
  type: user      -> facts about Jeff the person. True in every profile.

  type: project   -> current workstream state, carrying dates that expire
                     ("sendable ~Aug 26-28", "go/no-go 08-24"). Churns; the
                     churn is the concurrent-write hazard. LOCAL by default.
  type: reference -> pointers into external systems whose access is itself
                     profile-scoped (which MCPs are wired), and some are
                     secret-adjacent. Replicating them widens that surface for
                     little gain. LOCAL by default.

  Overrides, per file, in the frontmatter metadata block:
      sync: true    force a project/reference memory to travel
      sync: false   pin a feedback/user memory to its own profile

CONFLICT DOCTRINE — never trust a version header, only content:

  Both drifted files found on the first run carried metadata.modified stamps
  that could not be trusted (client-email-voice-deltas.md: IDENTICAL stamps,
  20KB of body missing on one side). So `modified` is reported as evidence for
  a human and is NEVER used to pick a winner. The ladder is:

    1. identical sha1                        -> no-op
    2. absent on a target                    -> ADD  (provably lossless)
    3. one side is a strict, ORDER-PRESERVING
       superset of another                   -> PROMOTE (provably lossless:
                                                every line of the loser
                                                survives, in order, and no
                                                inserted line negates one it
                                                keeps)
    4. anything else                         -> CONFLICT. No write, ever.
                                                Quarantined + filed to the
                                                board.

  Only 2 and 3 are applied unattended. Rule 4 is the unattended-judgment ban:
  an automated process may propagate an addition, never adjudicate ambiguity.
  superset() (2026-09-06, MEM-4) checks more than "every line of b is in a":
  it requires the extra lines to be pure insertions in a's own line order
  (difflib opcodes equal/insert only -- a reordered or edited copy is NOT a
  superset in either direction), and it refuses to promote an insertion that
  negation-flips a line it would otherwise keep ("Do not publish" + append
  "Publish automatically" is a CONFLICT, not a promotion).

  A memory that is doctrine-grade (eligible) in >=1 store and present with
  DIFFERENT content but marked ineligible (sync: false, or type: project /
  reference without an override) elsewhere is reported as a `divergent-
  ineligible` NOTE, never silently "clean" and never promoted into the store
  that opted out.

  Deletions never propagate. A file present in >=1 store is pushed to all; to
  retire a doctrine memory everywhere use `--retire <name>`.

  Index hygiene (2026-09-06, MEM-3): `--check` fails (exit >=1) on any index
  link with no corresponding file in the same store ("dangling"), and on any
  doctrine-grade memory present in a store with no index entry there
  ("unindexed") -- checked PER REGISTERED PROFILE, not main only (CD-R4-1:
  claudette alone carried 7 dangling links the first pass missed by checking
  main and generalizing). It WARNS (does not fail) when a profile's
  MEMORY.md exceeds the ~24,000-byte preload budget, listing the entries
  that sit past the boundary. Applying the sync itself only ever touches the
  ' · '-delimited segment belonging to the memory being synced on a
  multi-link index line -- never a sibling memory's link riding the same
  line (MEM-3: main's index used to carry three stale sibling segments this
  way onto claudine-only files).

Usage:
  memory-sync                 dry run: print the plan, write nothing (default)
  memory-sync --apply         snapshot all stores, then apply ADD/PROMOTE
  memory-sync --check         exit 1 if any sync-eligible drift or conflict
  memory-sync --scheduled     launchd mode: apply lossless, failtask the rest
  memory-sync --retire NAME   remove a memory + its index line from all stores
  memory-sync --verbose       show per-file evidence

Env:
  MEMORY_SYNC_HOME  overrides ~ for every store path (migration previews /
                    tests only -- never set this against a live profile).

Exit codes: 0 clean/applied · 1 drift present (--check) · 2 conflicts present
"""

